You generally **cannot attribute it causally** from those variables. Neither “typographical density” nor “pagination rhythm” is an established determinant of contract-claim settlement value, and controlling for filing fees does little to identify an effect. At most, formatting might proxy for readability, counsel quality, document complexity, or party resources.

If you merely want to estimate **associations**, you could specify:

\[
\ln S_i=\alpha+\beta_D\ln D_i+\beta_R\ln R_i+\gamma\ln F_{j(i)}
+X_i'\delta+\eta_{j(i)}+\varepsilon_i ,
\]

where:

- \(S_i\) is observed settlement value;
- \(D_i\) is an operationalized density measure, such as characters or words per page;
- \(R_i\) is a defined binder measure, such as variance in pages per exhibit or tabs per 100 pages;
- \(F_{j(i)}\) is the filing fee;
- \(\eta_{j(i)}\) represents jurisdictional fixed effects; and
- \(X_i\) includes claimed damages, liability strength, defenses, contract type, collectability, counsel, party characteristics, and litigation stage.

In that log-log specification, \(\beta_D\) and \(\beta_R\) are **estimated elasticities**: approximately, the percentage change in predicted settlement value associated with a 1% change in each formatting measure. They are not percentages of settlement value “caused” by each feature, nor can their relative sizes automatically be treated as causal attribution.

Causal attribution would require plausibly exogenous formatting variation—ideally randomized presentation of otherwise identical materials—plus attention to selection into settlement and the likelihood that formatting correlates with unobserved case complexity or advocate quality. Filing-fee controls alone cannot address those problems, and filing fees themselves may reflect amount in controversy or local procedural rules.

For practical valuation, I would assign these formatting features **no independent weight absent evidence**. The useful starting point is expected judgment—liability probability times recoverable damages—adjusted for litigation costs, timing, collectability, risk preferences, and bargaining leverage. Any readability or presentation effect should be treated as a secondary, empirically unproven adjustment.

“Differential indemnity decomposition” is not a universally standardized M&A term, so I would first confirm whether counsel means a **risk-allocation matrix** or a **quantitative expected-loss model**. Assuming the former, the right standard is **decision-useful granularity**, not a line-by-line diligence census.

**Split an exposure only when doing so could change a deal term or remediation decision**—for example, the cap, basket, survival period, escrow/holdback, special indemnity, purchase price, or representations-and-warranties insurance treatment.

For a mid-market SaaS target, that generally means:

| Exposure | Appropriate granularity |
|---|---|
| Ordinary business representations | Claim/category level; go deeper only for identified issues |
| Fundamental and tax matters | Separate categories, with known exposures itemized individually |
| **IP ownership and chain of title** | By material product, codebase, or patent family; then by exception involving founders, contractors, employees, prior employers, vendors, or acquired businesses |
| **Open-source and inbound third-party technology** | By license family for the portfolio, but component-level for core-product components with copyleft, unknown, incompatible, or missing commercial rights |
| **Infringement or misappropriation** | By affected product/IP family and relevant jurisdiction; claim-level for pending or threatened matters |
| **Customer and partner flow-down obligations** | Standard form versus deviations; contract-level for material agreements containing uncapped IP indemnities, exclusivity, assignment restrictions, source-code escrow, or unusual ownership provisions |
| Privacy, security, and AI/data rights | Separate branches where material to the product; incident-, regime-, or dataset-level for known issues |

For each decomposed exposure, capture only what supports a decision:

- the trigger and plausible loss pathway;
- affected IP, product, and revenue concentration;
- supporting diligence evidence and disclosure-schedule references;
- whether it is known, suspected, or unknown;
- proposed indemnity treatment and recovery source—seller, escrow/holdback, or insurance;
- insurance exclusions or collectability limitations;
- required pre-signing/pre-closing remediation and residual exposure.

### Where material IP concentration changes the analysis

Do not leave “IP indemnity” as one row. At minimum, separate **title/assignment defects, inbound licensing and open source, third-party infringement, and outbound customer commitments**. Those risks have different triggers, loss scenarios, evidence, and possible remedies.

Also account for **common-cause exposure**. A defective founder assignment or problematic license in the core repository could impair much of the platform and affect several contractual obligations simultaneously. Do not treat those rows as independent risks—or count the same revenue impairment multiple times. A concentrated-loss scenario is usually more informative than dozens of spuriously precise expected-loss estimates.

Item-level treatment is warranted for matters such as:

- missing assignments from core developers;
- unresolved prior-employer or contractor claims;
- core-code licenses incompatible with the proprietary SaaS model;
- actual or threatened infringement claims;
- material nonstandard customer indemnities; and
- other known matters likely to be excluded from insurance.

A practical deliverable could be **one compact indemnity matrix plus a focused IP annex**, rather than hundreds of rows. The stopping rule is simple: **if two items would receive the same treatment, rely on the same evidence, and produce no different negotiation or remediation decision, combine them.**

In short: **category-level for ordinary risks, asset/license/contract-exception level for concentrated IP, and item-level for known defects.** Deal size supports proportionality; it does not justify shallow analysis of the IP on which most of the target’s value depends.
